  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 1138 PM MDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nCurrent analysis shows the closed low centered near the New Mexico \nand southeastern Colorado border with ample moisture indicated by \nwater vapor imagery. Light showers continue over Lincoln, moderate \nto light over Washington, and light over far south/southeastern \nElbert county where dual pol storm total accumulations between the \ncommunities of Punkin Center and Kutch shows potentially near 4\" has \nfallen. Short term model guidance indicates showers should continue \nin our far east/southeastern counties until Thursday morning, and an \nadditional 0.25-0.50\" could fall, especially in Lincoln county. Some \nCAMs depict showers moving into the Denver metro as the night \ncontinues, but based on the CAMs recent performance we don't trust \nthese solutions very much. Nonetheless, we included a 15% chance for \na light shower to meager towards Denver through tonight. As the \nsystem pushes off to our southeast, cloud cover will clear across \nthe area as the day continues Thursday providing some true sunshine \nfor the first time in a few days. High temperatures Thursday will \nwarm into the lower 70s in the plains and the urban corridor. The \nmountains will see 50s and 60s.\n\nAfter a rather interesting and complicated forecast early this week, \nthe weather pattern for us turns much more straightforward for \nthis period. This weekend a ridge builds over the state favoring \ndry and above normal temperatures. Friday and Sunday will see \nsimilar temperatures with upper 70s/lower 80s across the I-25 \ncorridor, Denver metro, and eastern plains. Saturday will be a few\ndegrees warmer area wide in the low/mid 80s with some locations \nin the eastern plains reaching the upper 80s. Additionally, a weak\ncold front appears to makes its way through the northeastern \nplains Saturday afternoon causing breezy conditions with gusts in \nthe 20-30 kts range. This front will take down temperatures making\nfor a cooler Sunday for the far northeastern corner. \n\nMonday afternoon through Tuesday the patterns gets a touch more \ninteresting. Deterministic models shows a short wave moving across \nour area, and forecast soundings depict some moisture moving in \naloft. Tuesday should be the greatest chance in the higher terrain \nas the shortwave moves overhead and the moisture fills in. Tuesday \nwe currently have 20-30% across the high country. At this time \nensemble and deterministic guidance only shows a few members \nproviding rain for the I-25 and urban corridor. Forecast soundings \nalso indicate PWATs in the 0.6-0.7 range, but little low-level \nmoisture for the plains, so any confidence in measurable rain is \nlow at this time. Wednesday and Thursday the dry stagnant patterns\nlooks to return as the ridge develops overhead inducing upper 70s\nand lower 80s for the plains with 50s and 60s in the mountains.",
